const HTML: string = "<header id=\"top\" class=\"hero\"> <div class=\"hero-text\"> <h1 class=\"sr-only\">Re-Routing Devotion</h1> <p class=\"hero-sub\">A practical approach to managing holy waste on the Varanasi ghats</p> </div> <div class=\"hero-art\"> <img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/MSX4gZDbHieMabHan9unjfxvPs.webp\" width=\"2086\" height=\"1662\" alt=\"Cut-away render of the Ganga Kund built into the ghat steps, with its pump room, pools and a boat bay at the river\"> </div> </header> <section class=\"sec meta-sec\"> <div class=\"wrap\"> <dl class=\"meta\"> <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Collaborative project: Systems research, Strategic design, Service design, Giga mapping</dd></div> <div><dt>Context</dt><dd>Academic project, Human centered design for systems, strategies, services and speculations, IIT&nbsp;Kanpur</dd></div> <div><dt>Tools</dt><dd>Figma</dd></div> <div><dt>Outcome</dt><dd>Conceptual Ganga Kund and circular waste-management system for Varanasi's ghats</dd></div> </dl> <a class=\"feature\" href=\"https://www.rsd15.org//focuson2/re-routing-devotion\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/DTMCV3Vanc8Y3md8P4pMkGKvRc.png\" alt=\"RSD15\" width=\"34\" height=\"34\">Special feature in Relating Systems Design and Thinking symposium, 15th edition <span class=\"feat-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a> </div> </section> <section id=\"introduction\" class=\"sec\"> <div class=\"wrap wide\"> <figure class=\"giga-block\"> <button type=\"button\" class=\"giga\" data-open=\"giga\" aria-label=\"Open the full Giga Map viewer\"> <img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/IcxT9uRD5Pm7XJv2rFlofcA7oI.webp\" width=\"2600\" height=\"706\" alt=\"The complete Re-Routing Devotion Giga Map\" loading=\"lazy\"> </button> <figcaption><button type=\"button\" class=\"giga-btn\" data-open=\"giga\">See the full map <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></button><a class=\"jump-btn\" href=\"#insights\">Jump to insights <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↓</span></a></figcaption> </figure> </div> <div class=\"wrap intro-text\"> <h2><span class=\"zig\">Wicked problem</span> in question</h2> <p>Varanasi is considered one of the most visited cities in the world. The riverfront hosts rituals that have endured for centuries. People purchase offerings, make a prayer, and return sacred material to the Ganga. But many contemporary offerings now contain plastics, nylon, foil, synthetic coatings and other materials that do not belong in the river.</p> <p>It is easy to frame the visible outcome as a littering problem, but that overlooks the religious meaning of an offering and the difficult physical conditions under which waste must be collected.</p> </div> </section> <section id=\"scale\" class=\"sec alt\"> <div class=\"wrap\"> <h2>Scale of this challenge</h2> <div class=\"scale-row\"> <div class=\"metric\"> <p class=\"big\"><span data-count=\"70\">70</span><small>tonnes</small></p> <p>of floral waste generated across Varanasi every week.</p> </div> <figure class=\"buses-fig\" aria-label=\"70 tonnes is roughly 4 to 6 full-size buses\"> <div class=\"buses\"><svg class=\"bus\" viewBox=\"0 0 120 52\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 6h96c6 0 10 4 11 9l3 21v6c0 2-1 3-3 3H6c-2 0-3-1-3-3V9c0-2 1-3 3-3z\" class=\"b-body\"/><path d=\"M10 12h12v13H10zM25 12h12v13H25zM40 12h12v13H40zM55 12h12v13H55zM70 12h12v13H70z\" class=\"b-win\"/><path d=\"M88 12h14c3 0 5 2 6 5l2 14H88z\" class=\"b-win\"/><path d=\"M3 32h113\" class=\"b-line\"/><circle cx=\"26\" cy=\"45\" r=\"6.5\" class=\"b-tyre\"/><circle cx=\"26\" cy=\"45\" r=\"2.4\" class=\"b-hub\"/><circle cx=\"94\" cy=\"45\" r=\"6.5\" class=\"b-tyre\"/><circle cx=\"94\" cy=\"45\" r=\"2.4\" class=\"b-hub\"/></svg><svg class=\"bus\" viewBox=\"0 0 120 52\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 6h96c6 0 10 4 11 9l3 21v6c0 2-1 3-3 3H6c-2 0-3-1-3-3V9c0-2 1-3 3-3z\" class=\"b-body\"/><path d=\"M10 12h12v13H10zM25 12h12v13H25zM40 12h12v13H40zM55 12h12v13H55zM70 12h12v13H70z\" class=\"b-win\"/><path d=\"M88 12h14c3 0 5 2 6 5l2 14H88z\" class=\"b-win\"/><path d=\"M3 32h113\" class=\"b-line\"/><circle cx=\"26\" cy=\"45\" r=\"6.5\" class=\"b-tyre\"/><circle cx=\"26\" cy=\"45\" r=\"2.4\" class=\"b-hub\"/><circle cx=\"94\" cy=\"45\" r=\"6.5\" class=\"b-tyre\"/><circle cx=\"94\" cy=\"45\" r=\"2.4\" class=\"b-hub\"/></svg><svg class=\"bus\" viewBox=\"0 0 120 52\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 6h96c6 0 10 4 11 9l3 21v6c0 2-1 3-3 3H6c-2 0-3-1-3-3V9c0-2 1-3 3-3z\" class=\"b-body\"/><path d=\"M10 12h12v13H10zM25 12h12v13H25zM40 12h12v13H40zM55 12h12v13H55zM70 12h12v13H70z\" class=\"b-win\"/><path d=\"M88 12h14c3 0 5 2 6 5l2 14H88z\" class=\"b-win\"/><path d=\"M3 32h113\" class=\"b-line\"/><circle cx=\"26\" cy=\"45\" r=\"6.5\" class=\"b-tyre\"/><circle cx=\"26\" cy=\"45\" r=\"2.4\" class=\"b-hub\"/><circle cx=\"94\" cy=\"45\" r=\"6.5\" class=\"b-tyre\"/><circle cx=\"94\" cy=\"45\" r=\"2.4\" class=\"b-hub\"/></svg><svg class=\"bus\" viewBox=\"0 0 120 52\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 6h96c6 0 10 4 11 9l3 21v6c0 2-1 3-3 3H6c-2 0-3-1-3-3V9c0-2 1-3 3-3z\" class=\"b-body\"/><path d=\"M10 12h12v13H10zM25 12h12v13H25zM40 12h12v13H40zM55 12h12v13H55zM70 12h12v13H70z\" class=\"b-win\"/><path d=\"M88 12h14c3 0 5 2 6 5l2 14H88z\" class=\"b-win\"/><path d=\"M3 32h113\" class=\"b-line\"/><circle cx=\"26\" cy=\"45\" r=\"6.5\" class=\"b-tyre\"/><circle cx=\"26\" cy=\"45\" r=\"2.4\" class=\"b-hub\"/><circle cx=\"94\" cy=\"45\" r=\"6.5\" class=\"b-tyre\"/><circle cx=\"94\" cy=\"45\" r=\"2.4\" class=\"b-hub\"/></svg><svg class=\"bus maybe\" viewBox=\"0 0 120 52\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 6h96c6 0 10 4 11 9l3 21v6c0 2-1 3-3 3H6c-2 0-3-1-3-3V9c0-2 1-3 3-3z\" class=\"b-body\"/><path d=\"M10 12h12v13H10zM25 12h12v13H25zM40 12h12v13H40zM55 12h12v13H55zM70 12h12v13H70z\" class=\"b-win\"/><path d=\"M88 12h14c3 0 5 2 6 5l2 14H88z\" class=\"b-win\"/><path d=\"M3 32h113\" class=\"b-line\"/><circle cx=\"26\" cy=\"45\" r=\"6.5\" class=\"b-tyre\"/><circle cx=\"26\" cy=\"45\" r=\"2.4\" class=\"b-hub\"/><circle cx=\"94\" cy=\"45\" r=\"6.5\" class=\"b-tyre\"/><circle cx=\"94\" cy=\"45\" r=\"2.4\" class=\"b-hub\"/></svg><svg class=\"bus maybe\" viewBox=\"0 0 120 52\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 6h96c6 0 10 4 11 9l3 21v6c0 2-1 3-3 3H6c-2 0-3-1-3-3V9c0-2 1-3 3-3z\" class=\"b-body\"/><path d=\"M10 12h12v13H10zM25 12h12v13H25zM40 12h12v13H40zM55 12h12v13H55zM70 12h12v13H70z\" class=\"b-win\"/><path d=\"M88 12h14c3 0 5 2 6 5l2 14H88z\" class=\"b-win\"/><path d=\"M3 32h113\" class=\"b-line\"/><circle cx=\"26\" cy=\"45\" r=\"6.5\" class=\"b-tyre\"/><circle cx=\"26\" cy=\"45\" r=\"2.4\" class=\"b-hub\"/><circle cx=\"94\" cy=\"45\" r=\"6.5\" class=\"b-tyre\"/><circle cx=\"94\" cy=\"45\" r=\"2.4\" class=\"b-hub\"/></svg></div> <figcaption>70 tonnes (70,000 kg) equals roughly <b>4 to 6 full-size buses</b>.</figcaption> </figure> </div> <p class=\"lede\"><span class=\"u-red\">For a devotee, putting sacred material in a regular bin can feel like throwing devotion away.</span> For the sanitation worker, collecting waste on steep steps means carrying heavy, wet loads uphill through spaces inaccessible to conventional carts.</p> </div> </section> <section id=\"framing\" class=\"sec\"> <div class=\"wrap\"> <h2>Framing the system</h2> <p>To understand this problem, we decided to consider the old city of Varanasi. The old city consists of the major temples, old architecture and, most importantly, the ghats of the river Ganga.</p> <p>We followed the offering beyond the moment it enters the river. An object bought from a vendor becomes part of a ritual; after the ritual, it becomes material handled by people and institutions with very different priorities.</p> </div> <div class=\"wrap wide\"> <figure class=\"fig\"> <button type=\"button\" class=\"image-open\" data-img=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/YBJRauuBSl1ZxrStKD0MbUCw98.webp\" data-title=\"Framing the system\" data-caption=\"\"> <img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/YBJRauuBSl1ZxrStKD0MbUCw98.webp\" width=\"1920\" height=\"996\" alt=\"The research wall covered in maps, sticky notes and string connections\" loading=\"lazy\"> </button> </figure> </div> </section> <section id=\"timeline\" class=\"sec alt\"> <div class=\"wrap\"> <h2>What is the timeline we are considering our problem from?</h2> <p>While the city continued to grow and modernise, its religious traditions remained deeply rooted in everyday life. Today, ancient rituals coexist with dense urban development, mass pilgrimage and modern consumer materials.</p> </div> <div class=\"wrap wide\"> <ol class=\"tline\"> <li><h4>Traditional context</h4><span class=\"tl-dot\"></span><p>Natural materials, clay, leaves and metal offerings</p><img class=\"tl-icon\" src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/vX8pICVRf1SINM1BKCgW0ryAJHY.svg\" width=\"72\" height=\"38\" alt=\"\"></li> <li><h4>Material transition</h4><span class=\"tl-dot\"></span><p>Mass-produced objects and synthetic packaging become more common</p><img class=\"tl-icon\" src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/vT8Zrbo1g7nTcHid1HADFyMGzc.svg\" width=\"75\" height=\"34\" alt=\"\"></li> <li><h4>Present-day</h4><span class=\"tl-dot\"></span><p>Mixed waste, difficult segregation and disposal challenges</p><img class=\"tl-icon\" src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/c3q2nymUvWvyVbjVQnPGpGPM8.svg\" width=\"118\" height=\"35\" alt=\"\"></li> <li class=\"tl-float\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"tl-boat\"><img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/vX8pICVRf1SINM1BKCgW0ryAJHY.svg\" alt=\"\" class=\"on\"><img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/vT8Zrbo1g7nTcHid1HADFyMGzc.svg\" alt=\"\"><img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/c3q2nymUvWvyVbjVQnPGpGPM8.svg\" alt=\"\"></span></li> </ol> </div> </section> <section id=\"stakeholders\" class=\"sec\"> <div class=\"wrap\"> <h2>Who participates in the journey?</h2> </div> <div class=\"wrap wide\"> <figure class=\"fig clear stake-fig\"> <button type=\"button\" class=\"image-open\" data-img=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/KTAP2KzsdvoN58CCHmObYj7fXLA.webp\" data-title=\"Who participates in the journey?\" data-caption=\"\"> <img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/KTAP2KzsdvoN58CCHmObYj7fXLA.webp\" width=\"3643\" height=\"2435\" alt=\"Isometric ghat with stakeholders labelled at each level: primary at the water's edge, secondary on the middle steps, tertiary at the top\" loading=\"lazy\"> </button> </figure> </div> <div class=\"wrap\"> <ol class=\"tier-rows\" aria-label=\"Stakeholder tiers, from the top of the ghat to the water\"> <li class=\"tr t-3\"><div class=\"tr-k\"><i class=\"flag t3\"></i><span>Tertiary</span></div><div><h4>Systemic influence</h4><p>Government bodies, manufacturers, policymakers and broader institutions shape the conditions in which the system operates.</p></div></li> <li class=\"tr t-2\"><div class=\"tr-k\"><i class=\"flag t2\"></i><span>Secondary</span></div><div><h4>Operational influence</h4><p>Vendors, service providers and local management influence what enters the river and how waste is handled.</p></div></li> <li class=\"tr t-1\"><div class=\"tr-k\"><i class=\"flag t1\"></i><span>Primary</span></div><div><h4>Direct interaction</h4><p>Devotees, sanitation workers, boatmen and river life directly interact with the river and its immediate environment.</p></div></li> </ol> <p class=\"lede\">Although devotees perform the rituals, vendors, sanitation workers and governing bodies influence what enters the river and what happens afterward. The responsibility for managing sacred waste is therefore distributed across multiple stakeholders.</p> </div> </section> <section id=\"wicked\" class=\"sec alt\"> <div class=\"wrap\"> <h2>What makes this system so wicked in nature?</h2> <p class=\"pb-hint\">Drag the cards around :D</p> </div> <div class=\"wrap wide\"> <div class=\"pinboard\" data-widget=\"pinboard\"> <div class=\"pb-bar\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"pb-tidy\">Tidy up</button></div> <div class=\"pb-area\"> <article class=\"pcard\" data-i=\"0\"><img class=\"pin-m\" src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/Oe3PHFBQCiBPJMWiXijZ6YkCk0.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"43\" height=\"41\"><div class=\"pc-img\"><img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/Ypu1QDI8eNIphWItoqmc5K5g0.webp\" alt=\"A man bathing at the river’s edge among floating marigold offerings\" loading=\"lazy\" draggable=\"false\"></div><h4>Culture</h4><p>An offering can be simultaneously sacred to the person offering it and environmentally harmful in its material form.</p></article> <article class=\"pcard\" data-i=\"1\"><img class=\"pin-m\" src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/yixxbNrMO41k2B6LfwJJRdqOLBY.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"43\" height=\"41\"><div class=\"pc-img\"><img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/UAnAvOQssLYTD2FSHMR7dfPvS40.webp\" alt=\"Children on a stone ghat platform above water filled with plastic waste and flowers\" loading=\"lazy\" draggable=\"false\"></div><h4>Space</h4><p>Ghats are stepped, making them a difficult location for workers to collect, segregate and dispose of waste.</p></article> <article class=\"pcard\" data-i=\"2\"><img class=\"pin-m\" src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/60dnAIrnvFJzpWhNoCRpvNoDsP8.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"43\" height=\"41\"><div class=\"pc-img\"><img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/T9atKufK2QTjIRYZe3FPPvtFxT8.webp\" alt=\"A vendor’s sack of orange and yellow marigolds\" loading=\"lazy\" draggable=\"false\"></div><h4>Responsibility</h4><p>The material passes between devotees, vendors, workers, municipal services and potential processors, meaning no single actor governs the entire journey.</p></article> <svg class=\"pb-thread\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path class=\"th th-shadow\"/><path class=\"th th-shadow\"/><path class=\"th\"/><path class=\"th\"/></svg> <img class=\"pin\" src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/Oe3PHFBQCiBPJMWiXijZ6YkCk0.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"86\" height=\"83\" draggable=\"false\"> <img class=\"pin\" src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/yixxbNrMO41k2B6LfwJJRdqOLBY.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"86\" height=\"83\" draggable=\"false\"> <img class=\"pin\" src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/60dnAIrnvFJzpWhNoCRpvNoDsP8.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"86\" height=\"83\" draggable=\"false\"> </div> </div> </div> </section> <section id=\"translating\" class=\"sec\"> <div class=\"wrap\"> <h2>Translating the system</h2> <p>The research combined a frequency-versus-impact classification of materials, stakeholder relationships, a 24-hour waste cycle, an annual festival cycle and a larger Giga Map.</p> </div> <div class=\"wrap wide\"> <div class=\"bento-head\"> <p class=\"hint\">Select any map to open it full screen. Scroll or pinch to zoom, drag to pan, ← → to move between maps.</p> </div> <div class=\"bento\" id=\"bento\"></div> </div> <div class=\"wrap\"> <h3 class=\"sub\">Mapping Holy Waste - Frequency vs Impact</h3> <p>Flowers dominate the visible organic stream, but roughly one in four listed components was synthetic. Nylon garland threads, wrappers, foil and plaster-of-Paris objects were among the components that made source separation difficult.</p> </div> <div class=\"wrap wide\"> <div class=\"panel\" data-widget=\"matrix\"> <div class=\"pills matrix-filters\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Filter by material\"> <button class=\"is-on\" data-cat=\"all\">All</button> <button data-cat=\"organic\"><i class=\"sw organic\"></i>Organic</button> <button data-cat=\"synthetic\"><i class=\"sw synthetic\"></i>Synthetic &amp; textile</button> <button data-cat=\"chemical\"><i class=\"sw chemical\"></i>Dye, powder or coating</button> <button data-cat=\"inert\"><i class=\"sw inert\"></i>Metal &amp; objects</button> </div> <div class=\"matrix-scroll\"> <div class=\"matrix\" id=\"matrix\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Frequency versus impact matrix of offering components\"> <span class=\"ax ax-x-lo\">Low frequency</span><span class=\"ax ax-x-hi\">High frequency</span> <span class=\"ax ax-y-hi\">High impact</span><span class=\"ax ax-y-lo\">Low impact</span> </div> </div> <div class=\"panel-foot\"> <p class=\"matrix-readout\" aria-live=\"polite\">&nbsp;</p> <button type=\"button\" class=\"link\" data-open=\"matrix\">Open the original map ↗</button> </div> </div> </div> <div class=\"wrap\"> <h3 class=\"sub\">Contamination Composition</h3> <div class=\"comp\"> <figure class=\"comp-fig\" id=\"compFig\"> <img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/Wxlj0lRWbwIIEHwPV92I34u4g.webp\" width=\"392\" height=\"851\" alt=\"Stacked cylinder: glass and cloth at the top, then paper, plastics, and organic waste at the bottom\" loading=\"lazy\"> <span class=\"ct\" data-k=\"4\" style=\"--p:.094\"></span><span class=\"ct\" data-k=\"3\" style=\"--p:.168\"></span><span class=\"ct\" data-k=\"2\" style=\"--p:.271\"></span><span class=\"ct\" data-k=\"1\" style=\"--p:.43\"></span><span class=\"ct\" data-k=\"0\" style=\"--p:.717\"></span> </figure> <div> <dl class=\"comp-list\" id=\"compList\"> <div data-k=\"4\"><dt>Glass</dt><dd>4%</dd></div> <div data-k=\"3\"><dt>Cloth</dt><dd>6%</dd></div> <div data-k=\"2\"><dt>Paper</dt><dd>16%</dd></div> <div data-k=\"1\"><dt>Plastics</dt><dd>27%</dd></div> <div data-k=\"0\"><dt>Organic Waste</dt><dd>47%</dd></div> </dl> <p>The composition of ritual offerings has become increasingly complex with the introduction of synthetic and modern materials. However, traditional practices and existing infrastructure have not evolved to accommodate these changes.</p> </div> </div> </div> </section> <section id=\"annual-cycle\" class=\"sec alt\"> <div class=\"wrap\"> <h2>How Holy Waste Changes Through the Year - Low, Mid and Peak</h2> <p>We tracked the timeframe which Varanasi goes through every year. Mapping out the high and low points gives us clarity on whether the system operates temporally or consistently.</p> </div> <div class=\"wrap wide\"> <div class=\"panel\" data-widget=\"year\"> <div class=\"pills legend\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Filter by intensity\"> <button class=\"is-on\" data-level=\"all\">All</button> <button data-level=\"low\"><i class=\"sw low\"></i>Non-seasonal Low</button> <button data-level=\"mid\"><i class=\"sw mid\"></i>Transitional Mids</button> <button data-level=\"peak\"><i class=\"sw peak\"></i>Seasonal Peak</button> </div> <div class=\"year-track\"><div class=\"events\" role=\"tablist\" aria-label=\"Festivals and seasons\"></div><div class=\"months\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div></div> <div class=\"year-detail\" id=\"yearDetail\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div> <div class=\"panel-foot\"><span></span><button type=\"button\" class=\"link\" data-open=\"annual-cycle\">Open the original map ↗</button></div> </div> </div> <div class=\"wrap\"> <p class=\"lede\">The proposed collection system needs to handle occasional extreme demand and persistent everyday demand.</p> </div> </section> <section id=\"first-mile\" class=\"sec\"> <div class=\"wrap\"> <h2>The first mile</h2> </div> <div class=\"wrap wide\"> <div class=\"fmile\" data-widget=\"fmile\"> <svg class=\"fmile-svg\" viewBox=\"150 130 1900 900\" aria-hidden=\"true\"> <path class=\"fmile-path\" d=\"M235,697 C330,560 500,400 650,398 C710,397 760,420 806,445 C960,530 1150,570 1362,569 C1620,566 1850,480 1987,345\"/> <ellipse class=\"fmile-dot\" cx=\"235\" cy=\"697\" rx=\"21\" ry=\"16\"/> <ellipse class=\"fmile-dot\" cx=\"806\" cy=\"445\" rx=\"21\" ry=\"16\"/> <ellipse class=\"fmile-dot\" cx=\"1362\" cy=\"569\" rx=\"21\" ry=\"16\"/> <ellipse class=\"fmile-dot\" cx=\"1987\" cy=\"345\" rx=\"21\" ry=\"16\"/> </svg> <ol class=\"fmile-pts\"> <li class=\"fm-pt fm-a\" style=\"--l:7.05%;--t:62.2%\"><h4>Logistical Failures</h4><p>The first-mile transit relies entirely on slow, manual handcarts; heat-soaked flowers rot before they reach processing.</p></li> <li class=\"fm-pt fm-b\" style=\"--l:37.4%;--t:17.2%\"><h4>Spatial Chokepoints</h4><p>Ancient narrow alleys completely prevent access for standard municipal garbage trucks.</p></li> <li class=\"fm-pt fm-c\" style=\"--l:52.7%;--t:51.3%\"><h4>Material Reality</h4><p>10–18 tonnes of perishable florals daily, plus hidden synthetic plastics, ash, and chemical dyes.</p></li> <li class=\"fm-pt fm-d\" style=\"--l:72%;--t:2.2%;--w:18%\"><h4>Cascading Impact</h4><p>Ecological toxicity, economic vulnerability for vendors, and reputational damage for a sacred heritage city.</p></li> </ol> </div> </div> </section> <section id=\"ghats\" class=\"sec alt\"> <div class=\"wrap\"> <h2>The Different Ghats and their attributes</h2> <p>Each ghat serves a distinct purpose and attracts different patterns of activity. Our analysis suggested that waste accumulation is concentrated at specific ghats rather than evenly distributed, potentially affecting the river further downstream.</p> </div> <div class=\"wrap wide\"> <figure class=\"fig ghat-fig clear\"> <button type=\"button\" class=\"image-open\" data-img=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/8tAfaC6yNKq1d1tmznoeWUPLlU.webp\" data-title=\"The Different Ghats and their attributes\" data-caption=\"\"> <img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/8tAfaC6yNKq1d1tmznoeWUPLlU.webp\" width=\"2595\" height=\"2205\" alt=\"Radar charts for each ghat along the river, linked by lines to desk-research screenshots and sources for individual ghats\" loading=\"lazy\"> </button> </figure> </div> <div class=\"wrap\"> <div class=\"ghat-text\"> <p>For instance, Manikarnika Ghat primarily serves as a cremation site, resulting in a distinct waste composition dominated by cremation-related residues such as ash, wood remnants, flowers and other ritual materials.</p> </div> </div> </section> <section id=\"insights\" class=\"sec\"> <div class=\"wrap\"> <h2>Crack in the Ice - Insights</h2> <p>The research exposed a mismatch between how the system asks people to behave and what the environment allows them to do. We translated that mismatch into four design opportunities.</p> <ul class=\"rows-list\"> <li><h4>From disposal to ritual continuity</h4><p>The first problem was not the absence of bins alone. It was the gap between the meaning of an offering and the meaning of discarding it. <mark class=\"hl\">We needed a transition that could be perceived as the closing gesture of a ritual.</mark></p></li> <li><h4>From uphill hauling to gravity-assisted movement</h4><p>The existing first mile asks workers to move material against the terrain. The opportunity was to <mark class=\"hl\">redirect collection downhill</mark> and connect it to river-accessible transport.</p></li> <li><h4>From mixed waste to early separation</h4><p>Many low-mass synthetic components complicate processing once they are mixed with wet organic material. <mark class=\"hl\">Source-level separation could reduce that burden</mark>, although it would depend on appropriate use.</p></li> <li><h4>From a one-way flow to a material loop</h4><p>Offerings begin in local commerce and often end as unmanaged waste. <mark class=\"hl\">Processing suitable organic material into useful goods could create a possible path back into the local economy.</mark></p></li> </ul> </div> </section> <section id=\"how-might-we\" class=\"sec hmw-sec\"> <div class=\"wrap\"> <h2>How might we</h2> <p class=\"hmw\">Make the act of letting go feel ritual-compatible.</p> <div class=\"hmw-float\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/c3q2nymUvWvyVbjVQnPGpGPM8.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"118\" height=\"35\"></div> <p class=\"hmw\">Reduce the physical burden of moving waste up the steps.</p> </div> </section> <section id=\"collection\" class=\"sec\"> <div class=\"wrap\"> <div class=\"compare\" data-widget=\"compare\"> <div class=\"pills seg\" role=\"tablist\" aria-label=\"Collection approach\"> <button role=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\" data-side=\"new\">Top-down Approach <span class=\"tag new\">New</span></button> <button role=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"false\" data-side=\"current\">Bottom-Up Approach <span class=\"tag cur\">Current</span></button> </div> <div class=\"compare-body\"> <figure class=\"compare-fig\"><img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/8OvKqGLJY9wHC4pAAu8L2z0eLuA.webp\" width=\"535\" height=\"365\" alt=\"Diagram of ghat steps: the top-down route goes down to the river, the bottom-up route goes up to the street\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure> <div> <div class=\"cpanel\" data-side=\"new\"> <p><b>Route.</b> Sorted waste is pushed down the steps onto shallow barges waiting at the water's edge.</p> <p><b>Effort.</b> The river becomes a traffic-free highway: waste reaches processing faster, with far less manual hauling.</p> <p><b>Crowd.</b> Streets stay clear and collection happens out of sight, so devotees move uninterrupted.</p> </div> <div class=\"cpanel\" data-side=\"current\" hidden> <p><b>Route.</b> Heavy, wet waste is hauled up steep stairs, then carted through narrow, crowded alleys to the trucks.</p> <p><b>Effort.</b> Traffic and alley bottlenecks slow every trip, and the physical toll burns workers out fast.</p> <p><b>Crowd.</b> Carts clash with foot traffic, and devotees dodge leaking bins in a sacred space.</p> </div> </div> </div> </div> <h2 class=\"mt\">Top-Down Approach</h2> <ol class=\"chain\" aria-label=\"Parts that depend on one another\"> <li>The Kund</li><li>Transfer system</li><li>Barge operations</li><li>Processing partners</li><li>Local vendors</li> </ol> <p class=\"chain-txt\">These parts depend on one another. If one handoff fails, the loop is interrupted.</p> <p class=\"chain-note\">The intervention is therefore a proposal about relationships and operations as much as product form.</p> <ul class=\"dash plain\"> <li><b>The Accessibility Problem.</b> The river is used as a bypass, eliminating the need for vehicles to travel through Varanasi's inaccessible narrow alleyways.</li> <li><b>The Labor Burden.</b> It substitutes gravity to eliminate the uphill hauling of waste to drain, for a smooth downward slide to water-level barges.</li> <li><b>The Space Conflict.</b> It shifts the mess of logistics to the water, leaving the spiritual experience of devotees on the ghats entirely undisturbed.</li> </ul> </div> </section> <section id=\"ganga-kund\" class=\"sec alt\"> <div class=\"wrap\"> <h2>Intervention - The Ganga Kund</h2> <div class=\"kund-intro\"> <p>Conceptualised as a response to the shifting infrastructure of Varanasi, the Ganga Kund acts as a centralized point for pilgrims to dispose of holy offerings. The project explores the union of religious behavior and architectural design, specifically addressing how pilgrims distinguish between the sacred and the profane.</p> <p>Organic offerings and dry synthetics are intended to be segregated at the deposit point, reducing the complexity of sorting mixed material later.</p> </div> </div> <div class=\"wrap wide\"> <div class=\"kund kund-big\" data-widget=\"kund\"> <div class=\"kund-stage\"> <img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/vJArRskH6yd4rL2iCxm0NqJgadU.webp\" width=\"2680\" height=\"1814\" alt=\"Cut-away render of the Ganga Kund with numbered markers on its parts\" loading=\"lazy\"> <button class=\"hot\" style=\"--x:18%;--y:13%\" data-k=\"0\" aria-label=\"Note 1\"><span>1</span></button> <button class=\"hot\" style=\"--x:41%;--y:41%\" data-k=\"1\" aria-label=\"Note 2\"><span>2</span></button> <button class=\"hot\" style=\"--x:23%;--y:35%\" data-k=\"2\" aria-label=\"Note 3\"><span>3</span></button> <button class=\"hot\" style=\"--x:33%;--y:47%\" data-k=\"3\" aria-label=\"Note 4\"><span>4</span></button> <button class=\"hot\" style=\"--x:33%;--y:30%\" data-k=\"4\" aria-label=\"Note 5\"><span>5</span></button> <button class=\"hot\" style=\"--x:54%;--y:64%\" data-k=\"5\" aria-label=\"Note 6\"><span>6</span></button> </div> <ol class=\"notes-list\"> <li data-k=\"0\"><span>1</span><p>By aligning the physical structure with the spiritual expectations of the user, the design prevents accidents and misuse without disrupting the ritual flow.</p></li> <li data-k=\"1\"><span>2</span><p>A continuous flow of water extends from the river to the deposit point, creating a natural connection between the Ganga Kund and the Ganga.</p></li> <li data-k=\"2\"><span>3</span><p>An internal pump room draws river water to create a continuous, recirculating current.</p></li> <li data-k=\"3\"><span>4</span><p>Integrated into the vertical slots of the structure, these cartridges use activated charcoal and river gravel. These natural elements act as a \"ritualistic bypass,\" filtering out fine particles without using harsh chemicals.</p></li> <li data-k=\"4\"><span>5</span><p>The Kund is designed to accommodate several events such as Deep Daan, small idol immersion, etc.</p></li> <li data-k=\"5\"><span>6</span><p>Taking into consideration our top-to-bottom method of waste collection and transport, the offerings become easy to collect via boats which can be accessed on the river.</p></li> </ol> </div> </div> <div class=\"wrap wide\"> <div class=\"gallery\"> <button type=\"button\" class=\"g g-a image-open\" data-group=\"kund\" data-img=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/EAiluQxrrlLNVgFRDtrnGLufVZY.webp\" data-title=\"Intervention - The Ganga Kund\" data-caption=\"\"><img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/EAiluQxrrlLNVgFRDtrnGLufVZY.webp\" alt=\"Render of the Ganga Kund pool with people at the railing\" loading=\"lazy\"></button> <button type=\"button\" class=\"g g-b image-open\" data-group=\"kund\" data-img=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/6RAODOxkF9HmdVcU2WP3X2ms.webp\" data-title=\"Intervention - The Ganga Kund\" data-caption=\"\"><img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/6RAODOxkF9HmdVcU2WP3X2ms.webp\" alt=\"Render of the Kund's boat bay at river level\" loading=\"lazy\"></button> <button type=\"button\" class=\"g g-c image-open\" data-group=\"kund\" data-img=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/I2kRRcEUxl17rBvI2KFQnSBOU8.webp\" data-title=\"Intervention - The Ganga Kund\" data-caption=\"\"><img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/I2kRRcEUxl17rBvI2KFQnSBOU8.webp\" alt=\"Diyas floating on the Kund's water\" loading=\"lazy\"></button> <button type=\"button\" class=\"g g-d image-open\" data-group=\"kund\" data-img=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/tnNHwcCfhJaTAOTaotmCWPsgNA.webp\" data-title=\"Intervention - The Ganga Kund\" data-caption=\"\"><img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/tnNHwcCfhJaTAOTaotmCWPsgNA.webp\" alt=\"Angled render of the Kund pool and railing\" loading=\"lazy\"></button> <button type=\"button\" class=\"g g-e image-open\" data-group=\"kund\" data-img=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/H4hWr0A8OUTeJ0uCkS470g8jVs4.webp\" data-title=\"Intervention - The Ganga Kund\" data-caption=\"\"><img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/H4hWr0A8OUTeJ0uCkS470g8jVs4.webp\" alt=\"Isometric section of the transfer from the Kund to a boat\" loading=\"lazy\"></button> <button type=\"button\" class=\"g g-f image-open\" data-group=\"kund\" data-img=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/5CmKtyeS2BlyxZZrchGnYuF6s.webp\" data-title=\"Intervention - The Ganga Kund\" data-caption=\"\"><img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/5CmKtyeS2BlyxZZrchGnYuF6s.webp\" alt=\"Isometric view of Kunds along the ghat steps\" loading=\"lazy\"></button> </div> </div> <div class=\"wrap\"> <h3 class=\"sub\">A few considerations on designing a Holy offering deposit site</h3> <p>To design for such a sensitive context, we studied how objects acquire sacred meaning. <em>The Sacred and the Profane</em>, Moral Disgust Theory and the Contagion Heuristic helped us understand religious perceptions and inform our intervention.</p> <p class=\"lede sm\">During aarti, festivals, and peak pilgrimage, cognitive load is at maximum. People are not thinking analytically. Any behavior that requires reading instructions, opening a lid, or making a choice will be unlikely.</p> <ol class=\"principles\"> <li>Place units where people naturally pause or slow down. Make the opening gesture effortless.</li> <li>Design the moment of use to feel like completion of a rite.</li> <li>Use language and form that frames the act as completion or return, not disposal.</li> <li>The unit must look like it could have existed for centuries.</li> <li>Communicate the afterlife of the material.</li> <li>The deposit gesture must feel like an offering.</li> <li>Map placement on the sacred–profane axis.</li> <li>The two openings must be physically, visually, and symbolically distinct.</li> </ol> </div> <div class=\"wrap\"> <div class=\"signage\"> <figure class=\"fig sign-fig\"> <button type=\"button\" class=\"image-open\" data-group=\"kund\" data-img=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/3DVbxTdMMwbkxIgagO7IPAYe2U.webp\" data-title=\"Intervention - The Ganga Kund\" data-caption=\"\"> <img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/3DVbxTdMMwbkxIgagO7IPAYe2U.webp\" width=\"2800\" height=\"1273\" alt=\"Yellow bilingual signboard 'Guidelines of use' listing allowed and prohibited offerings\" loading=\"lazy\"> </button> </figure> <p>Appropriate signage will be set up so that the sanctity of the Ganga Kund is maintained and that the flow of offerings isn't mixed with external materials.</p> </div> <p class=\"lede\">A proposed ritual-integrated deposit station, supported by source separation, gravity-assisted transfer, floating barges and potential downstream processing.</p> </div> </section> <section id=\"circular-economy\" class=\"sec\"> <div class=\"wrap\"> <h2>Circular Economy</h2> <p>This circular economy model intercepts traditional religious offerings at specialized Ganga Kunds, preventing direct river pollution. Collected by boat and transported upstream, the organic waste is separated and upcycled into valuable resources: flowers become incense and compost, coconuts yield activated carbon, and fabrics are transformed into biodegradable packaging.</p> </div> <div class=\"wrap wide\"> <figure class=\"fig clear\"> <button type=\"button\" class=\"image-open\" data-group=\"ce\" data-img=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/Ko2PxuF6eiAftdXYH5HSotwaLYI.svg\" data-title=\"Circular Economy\" data-caption=\"\"> <img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/Ko2PxuF6eiAftdXYH5HSotwaLYI.svg\" width=\"1692\" height=\"846\" alt=\"Loop diagram: supply to the vendors, purchase, offering, Ganga Kund, collection, transport, produce and back to the shops\" loading=\"lazy\"> </button> </figure> <figure class=\"fig clear\"> <button type=\"button\" class=\"image-open\" data-group=\"ce\" data-img=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/2Ezx6KGfq2A7IEIm8CMv6dsg.webp\" data-title=\"Circular Economy\" data-caption=\"\"> <img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/2Ezx6KGfq2A7IEIm8CMv6dsg.webp\" width=\"5281\" height=\"1753\" alt=\"Illustrated loop: shop, temple, ghat and Kund, boats, collection truck and recycling unit\" loading=\"lazy\"> </button> </figure> </div> </section> <section id=\"system-dependencies\" class=\"sec alt\"> <div class=\"wrap\"> <h2>Why a system, not a single object?</h2> <p class=\"lede\">The Kund, transfer system, barge operations, processing partners and local vendors depend on one another. If one handoff fails, the loop is interrupted. The intervention is therefore a proposal about relationships and operations as much as product form.</p> </div> </section> <section id=\"reflection\" class=\"sec\"> <div class=\"wrap wide\"> <article class=\"know-card\"> <figure class=\"know-img\"><img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/pBNqFBZPqWNReeyWD0gGgdyGFw.webp\" width=\"2400\" height=\"1624\" alt=\"Aerial view of the Varanasi ghats and the Ganga, with moored boats along the steps and the sandbank across the river\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure> <div class=\"know-body\"> <h2>What we know and what we don't</h2> <p>Our research was entirely desk-based, drawing on published studies, calculated values and assumption-based estimates. Without interviews, field surveys or direct measurements, our maps highlight potential relationships and risks rather than validated outcomes.</p> <p>While existing Ganga trash skimmers, floral-waste upcycling initiatives and CPCB guidelines establish precedents, they do not validate our proposed intervention. The Ganga Kund's cultural acceptance, technical feasibility, financial viability and resilience to flooding remain areas for further testing.</p> </div> </article> </div> <div class=\"wrap\"> <h2 class=\"mt\">Reflection</h2> <p>Wicked problems don't take binary answers. Balancing an ancient city's physical limits against what people believe meant we couldn't force a logistics fix that ignored belief. The maps show where the system breaks. They can't tell us whether a devotee will use the vessel we drew; that question belongs to the ghats.</p> <h2 class=\"mt\">Conclusion</h2> <p>We believe that the graphic-led deposit station could adapt to other crowded pilgrimage sites, subject to local norms. Whether the loop closes at the cost and reliability it needs is a question this paper doesn't settle. What the map offers is an account of where the system breaks and which interventions are worth testing first, and a case that tradition and the river aren't opposed.</p> </div> </section> <section id=\"authorship\" class=\"sec alt\"> <div class=\"wrap\"> <p class=\"closing\">The ambition was not to make people choose between faith and sustainability. It was to explore whether the journey of an offering could honour both.</p> </div> <div class=\"wrap wide\"> <figure class=\"fig end-photo\"> <button type=\"button\" class=\"image-open\" data-img=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/uzJevpolRfVPNxQw0JHyPJd49U.webp\" data-title=\"Authorship\" data-caption=\"\"> <img src=\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/uzJevpolRfVPNxQw0JHyPJd49U.webp\" width=\"1895\" height=\"984\" alt=\"Three team members working at the research wall\" loading=\"lazy\"> </button> </figure> </div> <div class=\"wrap\"> <div class=\"credits\"> <div> <h2>Authorship</h2> <p>Bhargav Ram Chodabattula, Manvi Bhayre, Yash Maherwal and Vivek Kant — Indian Institute of Technology Kanpur, India.</p> <p class=\"small\">The project's findings and design intentions come from the RSD15 paper, \"Re-Routing Devotion: A practical approach to managing holy waste on the Varanasi ghats.\" All design imagery is reproduced from that paper. The proposed Ganga Kund was not represented as a tested or implemented system.</p> <a class=\"paper-link\" href=\"https://www.rsd15.org//focuson2/re-routing-devotion\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Read the paper on RSD15 <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a> </div> <div> <h2>References</h2> <ul class=\"refs\"> <li>Atal, H. L. (2022). Sustainable management of floral waste to produce bioenergy and valuable products.</li> <li>Kamboj, V. et al. (2024). Characterization and management of solid waste at selected spiritual sites in Dehradun.</li> <li>Kumar, A. &amp; Rana, N. K. (2024). Source segregation and solid waste management in Varanasi.</li> <li>Mishra, S. et al. (2025). Work-related musculoskeletal disorders among occupational workers in India.</li> <li>Singh, P. K. et al. (2024). Microplastics in the Ganga River ecosystem of Varanasi.</li> <li>Rozin, P., Millman, L. &amp; Nemeroff, C. (1986). Laws of sympathetic magic and purity heuristics.</li> <li>Sinha, A. (2018). Ghats on the Ganga: a sustainable model for waste management.</li> <li>Central Pollution Control Board (2020). Revised Guidelines for Idol Immersion.</li> </ul> <details class=\"all-refs\"><summary>All 24 references from the paper</summary><ul class=\"refs small\" id=\"allRefs\"></ul></details> </div> </div> </div> </section>"
